{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a0341d7d3bf1e8136","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01YVWdKEQN8gJaNH8hEvMTG4","description":"Write misc/internal error tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","is_backgrounded":true,"spawn_depth":1,"task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-GjGJaF\nCrate dir:   /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-GjGJaF/translation\nNEVER modify anything under c_src/. NEVER modify tests/common/*, tests/gen_api.py, any other tests/t*.rs, src/**, or Cargo.toml.\nYour ONLY output file: translation/tests/t24_err_misc.rs\n\nCONTEXT: differential C-vs-Rust verification of libpng 1.6.59.\n`translation/ERRORS.md` is the error-surface table. Your file covers the rows\nfrom `c_src/src/png.c`, `c_src/src/pngerror.c`, `c_src/src/pngmem.c`,\n`c_src/src/pngrio.c` and `c_src/src/pngwio.c` that are NOT already covered by\n`tests/t23_err_write.rs` (read that file first to see what it already does:\ncreate-struct version checks, the error/warning dispatchers, png_set_longjmp_fn,\npng_malloc sizes, png_set_option, png_permit_mng_features).\n\nMost of your targets are exported internal helpers that return a SENTINEL rather\nthan longjmp'ing, so they can be tested IN-PROCESS.\n\n## Harness (read these first)\n- translation/tests/common/mod.rs      -- types, PNG_* constants, `Rng`,\n  `eq_dbg`, `eq_bytes`, `cstr_to_string`\n- translation/tests/common/api.rs      -- `apis()` -> `.c` / `.rs` `Api` structs.\n  It ALREADY includes the internal symbols you need: png_icc_check_header,\n  png_icc_check_length, png_icc_check_tag_table (check api.rs; if one is missing\n  resolve it yourself with `common::sym::<T>(&libs().c, \"name\")` — do NOT edit\n  api.rs), png_zstream_error, png_muldiv, png_reciprocal, png_reciprocal2,\n  png_gamma_significant, png_safecat, png_format_number, png_check_fp_number,\n  png_check_fp_string, png_ascii_from_fp, png_ascii_from_fixed, png_fixed,\n  png_fixed_ITU, png_check_IHDR, png_check_keyword, png_chunk_unknown_handling,\n  png_handle_as_unknown, png_reset_zstream, png_malloc_base, png_malloc_array,\n  png_realloc_array, png_zalloc, png_zfree, png_XYZ_from_xy, png_xy_from_XYZ,\n  png_user_version_check, png_warning_parameter*, png_formatted_warning,\n  png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer, png_convert_from_struct_tm.\n- translation/tests/common/harness.rs  -- in-process recorder AND the\n  sub-process driver (`child_case`, `set_child_mode`, `emit`, `child_finish`,\n  `diff_case`, `run_child`)\n- translation/tests/t23_err_write.rs   -- the sub-process template\n- translation/tests/t01_low_level.rs   -- the in-process pure-function template\n\nStart with `mod common;` then\n`use common::api::{apis, Api}; use common::harness::*; use common::*;`\n\n## What to cover\n1. **png_ascii_from_fp / png_ascii_from_fixed with a TOO-SMALL buffer** ->\n   `\"ASCII conversion buffer too small\"` (there are TWO such sites in each\n   function; find them in png.c). This is a `png_error`, so use the SUB-PROCESS\n   mechanism. Cover buffer sizes 0..=16 x a range of values and precisions.\n   Allocate the real Rust buffer generously and pass a SMALL `size` argument so\n   there is no actual overflow in the test.\n2. **png_fixed / png_fixed_ITU out of range** -> `\"fixed point overflow in <text>\"`.\n   Sub-process. Cover values just inside and just outside the limits, plus NaN,\n   +/-inf, f64::MAX, f64::MIN, 0.0, -0.0.\n3. **png_muldiv / png_reciprocal / png_reciprocal2 overflow sentinels**\n   (return 0 / return 0 fixed point). In-process; use many randomised i32\n   triples with a fixed seed plus all the boundary values\n   (0, +/-1, i32::MIN, i32::MAX).\n4. **png_XYZ_from_xy / png_xy_from_XYZ**: every `return 1` failure branch —\n   the `fpLimit = 110000` range checks, `whitey < 5`, the denominators that go\n   to zero, and the png_muldiv overflows. In-process, comparing the return code\n   AND the output struct (only when the return says success). Define the\n   `png_xy` / `png_XYZ` repr(C) structs yourself (see tests/t01_low_level.rs).\n   Drive with a large randomised sweep (fixed seed) plus targeted values around\n   110000, 0, 5 and i32 extremes.\n5. **png_zstream_error**: all TEN distinct message strings. Call it with every\n   zlib return code (Z_OK=0, Z_STREAM_END=1, Z_NEED_DICT=2, Z_ERRNO=-1,\n   Z_STREAM_ERROR=-2, Z_DATA_ERROR=-3, Z_MEM_ERROR=-4, Z_BUF_ERROR=-5,\n   Z_VERSION_ERROR=-6) and also out-of-range codes (-7, -100, 2, 100, i32::MIN,\n   i32::MAX), then read back `png_ptr->zstream.msg`. You cannot see the struct\n   field directly, so instead trigger the message through a path that reports\n   it, OR compare it indirectly: after `png_zstream_error`, provoke a\n   diagnostic that includes the message. If neither is feasible, drive the codes\n   through `png_reset_zstream` / a corrupt-zlib read instead and compare the\n   resulting error text; explain your approach in a file comment.\n6. **png_icc_check_header / _length / _tag_table**: EVERY rejection reason\n   (there are ~22 in png.c, distinguishing the four that only WARN and return 1\n   from the ones that return 0). Build ICC profiles byte-by-byte in the test:\n   vary the length field, the signature at offset 36 (`acsp`), the device class\n   at 12, the colour space at 16, the PCS at 20, the version at 8, the rendering\n   intent at 64, the PCS illuminant at 68..80 (D50 = 0x0000f6d6, 0x00010000,\n   0x0000d32d), the tag count at 128, and the tag table entries. Drive them both\n   directly (in-process, comparing the int return AND the warning transcript)\n   AND through a crafted `iCCP` chunk read with the simplified API (see\n   tests/t20_err_read.rs `simple_read` for that idiom — reimplement it locally,\n   do not modify t20).\n7. **png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer**: all NINE `return 0` field checks\n   (year > 9999, month == 0, month > 12, day == 0, day > 31, hour > 23,\n   minute > 59, second > 60, out == NULL). In-process; exhaustive over the\n   boundaries plus a big randomised sweep.\n8. **png_user_version_check**: every mismatch shape -> the\n   `\"Application built with libpng-... but running with ...\"` warning and\n   `return 0`. Sub-process, because the caller then returns NULL.\n9. **png_malloc_array / png_realloc_array**: the `return NULL` sentinels,\n   including `nelements <= 0`, an element size of 0, the\n   `INT_MAX - old_elements` overflow guard, and sizes that exceed PNG_SIZE_MAX.\n   Also `\"internal error: array alloc\"` / `\"internal error: array realloc\"`\n   (sub-process).\n10. **png_zalloc / png_zfree**: zero items, zero size, huge products that\n    overflow `unsigned int`, and `png_zfree(NULL)`.\n11. **png_safecat / png_format_number / png_check_fp_number /\n    png_check_fp_string** boundary and rejection cases NOT already covered by\n    tests/t01_low_level.rs — read t01 first and only add what it does not do\n    (e.g. `png_format_number` with an out-of-range `format` value, and\n    `png_check_fp_number` with an out-of-range initial `state`).\n12. **png_check_keyword**: empty keyword, leading/trailing/multiple spaces,\n    control characters, non-Latin-1 bytes, a keyword longer than 79 bytes, and\n    the exact returned length + rewritten keyword bytes.\n13. **png_check_IHDR** called directly with every invalid combination — all 19\n    warning sites plus the terminal `\"Invalid IHDR data\"` (sub-process).\n14. **png_chunk_unknown_handling / png_handle_as_unknown** with every\n    `png_set_keep_unknown_chunks` configuration and many chunk names, including\n    names with invalid bytes.\n15. **png_reset_zstream** with a struct where the zstream was never\n    initialised, and after a successful/failed read.\n16. **png_rio / png_wio**: `\"Call to NULL read function\"` and `\"Read Error\"`\n    (pngrio.c) and their write counterparts (pngwio.c). Check the C first to see\n    which are actually reachable through the public API — if setting a NULL\n    function makes libpng install the stdio default instead (as it does on the\n    write side), say so in a comment and cover only the reachable ones.\n    Also cover the cross-wired warning (\"Read/write function ... \" in both\n    files) that fires when a read function is set on a write struct.\n\n## Rules\n- Before adding a NULL-pointer or extreme-value case, CHECK THE C SOURCE that a\n  guard exists. If the C dereferences with no check, that input is UB and must\n  NOT be tested — add a comment citing the C file:line instead. For in-process\n  tests UB shows up as a SIGSEGV that kills the whole test binary; for\n  sub-process tests `diff_case` panics with\n  \"died from signal ... in BOTH libraries without emitting anything\".\n- Fixed seeds; reproducible; total runtime under ~200 seconds.\n- Build/iterate:\n    cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-GjGJaF/translation && \\\n      timeout 600 cargo build --release --test t24_err_misc 2>&1 | tail -40\n    timeout 600 cargo test --release --test t24_err_misc -- --test-threads=4 2>&1 | \\\n      grep -vE 'Box<dyn Any>|panicked at src/pngerror' | \\\n      grep -E 'test result|MISMATCH|ERROR-PATH|died from|FAILED' -A12 | head -40\n- Iterate until ALL tests in your file PASS.\n- Include a `#[test] fn self_check()` proving the comparison is not vacuous:\n  assert that a set of specific expected message strings / sentinels really do\n  appear in the C results.\n\nReport: number of #[test] functions, number of individual cases, the final\n`test result:` line verbatim, every C location where you found no guard, which\nof the 16 items above you could NOT cover and exactly why, and any genuine\nC-vs-Rust divergence.","uuid":"9e906e86-2b7e-4227-8554-207c58b50c4a","session_id":"d61bc42a-4e8d-424f-b737-52a7165119c7"}
